issues there  in a millimeter in diameter or perhaps a  few hundred microns in diameter and then  that leaves you with all of the lower  range of microfluidic length scales like  tens or hundreds of microns to control  the environment of the tissue so we're  able to for example deliver streams of  fluid locally to subunits of tissue or  to connect multiple tissues together and  those are things I'm going to share with  you later in the talk  the combination of ex-vivo slices and  micro fluidics has been around for  perhaps 15 years and it really began  with brain slices that makes sense  because brain was the first organ that  was really sliced frequently if I know  scientists and for decades now they have  been collecting and culturing tissue  slices either acute for just a few hours  to study the electrochemistry or even  for months in order to study development  of the brain and micro fluidics engineer  has quickly figured out that by using  fluidic control at the micro scale they  could more efficiently in control  nutrient and waste exchange and they  could control the gas environment around  the tissue often in a way that was more  controlled than in classic cell culture  dishes but we can go far beyond that by  using micro fluidics you can deliver to  just one part of the tissues shown there  in the orange circle while preserving  the ability to interface the device with  imaging and other and analytical  modalities and so this type of  technology has been applied by a handful  of groups around the world this is still  a small field and it would be exciting  if some of the of you listening today  decided to join this area and so used to  give you a sense of what is possible I'm  showing you four different types of  applications that have been pursued so  far we see some drug and toxicity  testing in the upper left that came out  of sabath their porches group in the  Netherlands back in 2010 where they  actually connected multiple types of  organs together I believe it was liver  and gut in order to look at metabolic  changes in response to drugs we've also  had mechanistic experiments where  microchips that were interfaced with  electrochemical arrays were used to map  out signaling in the brain there's  applications in personalized medicine  for example taking patient tumor samples  and putting them onto a chip in order to  test different combinations of  chemotherapy and that came from the  Gervaise lab in Montreal and then  interestingly a six-month-long culture  of testes tissue is shown in the lower  right and they were actually able to  guess from out of genesis and then breed  mice using the sperm that were produced  so this was highly viable tissue culture  for over six months and that came out of  Japan just a couple of years ago and so  you can see that there's a lot of  potential for using this type of  technology and combining the intact  tissue with its spatial organization  with the fluidics  what my lab works on is taking the  technology and using it to build new  tools for the study of the immune system  this is an area where there is there's a  lot of need for new tools in the field  of immunology  especially ones that look at spatial  structure as most of the advances in  immunology lately have come from either  single style analyses or flow cytometry  or in vivo imaging where there isn't  much control over what's happening in  the tissue at any given time and it's  mostly watching through window and so by  combining micro fluidics and chemistry  and immunology we hope to develop new  tools so that we can ask questions about  how your immune system is organized and  how that drives the spatial effects or  how that drives the the response in an  immune event so just very briefly orient  you on the tissue that you're going to  see featured in the rest of the top but  then we'll get back to  technology my lab really focuses on an  organ called the lymph node and you may  be familiar when that Wigan it's your  tonsils for example which gets swollen  when you have an infection and this is  an organ that is highly structured it's  similar to a brain where there are sub  structures that are responsible for one  function or another such as antibody  development or the development of killer  cells and they're scattered throughout  your body and strategic locations so  that you can respond appropriately to  infections in different parts of your  body when my lab has been developing for  the past few years is a method to  collect the lymph nodes from mice and  then slice those and we've tested  extensively the viability and the immune  function of those tissues ex vivo in  order to ensure that they can still  respond to inflammatory stimuli or other  immune stimuli and we have found that a  fly store like the one shown here still  preserves the spatial organization that  we would expect to see in vivo what  you're looking at is a piece of Mouse  lymph node that is alive and Culture and  we've labeled in green where the b-cells  are and in blue where the lymphatic  vessels are and we can see this  beautiful structure is preserved ex vivo  okay a with this technology what are  some of the things that are possible  what I'm going to share with you are two  short stories one using tissue slices in  fluidics to ask questions about local  events in the tissue and then the other  to ask about interactions between  multiple organs in the body so two  different levels of spatial organization  and we're going to start with local  stimulation okay so the question that  really drove me when I started working  on this several years ago was how two  local events trigger functions in the  body and again here we're talking about  immunity but this is just as real  in other organs such as the brain or the  gut or the lung that you may be  interested in and so we wanted to ask  what happens if you deliver a drug to  one part of an organism no there for  example how quickly does it spread  through the tissue and does that vary by  by area of the organ and then later we  might be able to ask questions about  triggering events and propagation  through tissue but how do you deliver to  just one part of a piece of tissue that  is only millimeters in diameter you can  imagine that pipetting here is quite  difficult especially when this is an  ambassador and we didn't want to insert  any micro needles into the tissue as  that that may damage it and cause there  to be a reaction that changes the  response so we took a cue from some  prior work out of David Eddington's lab  at the University of illinois-chicago  where he had laid a microfluidic channel  beneath the chamber holding a brain  slice and then used ports to deliver to  just one part of the brain tissue as  illustrated here and so what we did was  we adapted this down for the lymph node  and you can see the device design here  so there are three layers to this  microfluidic chip the bottom layer has  an array of channels the middle layer  has an array of ports where there's one  port per channel and then the upper  layer is simply a culture chamber where  you can lay your tissue and so the  concept is that you would choose a  feature of the tissue that you want to  deliver drug to for example maybe that  red area of the tissue is interesting  and then you would choose the channel  that has a port that terminates beneath  that region and deliver drug to it as  shown there in orange so we construction  this device out of three layers right  now it's just being out of  polydimethylsiloxane or PDMS using  standard photo lithography methods and  you see the image of the chip there on  the right with its large central  circular chamber where you can lay the  tissue and if we zoom in there  we can see the array of channels in the  lower layer and the array of ports on  top and you see that the ports are  scattered that's to increase the chance  that they fall under a region of  interest in the tissue so I want to show  you what it looks like when we do local  delivery we have here a fluorescent  image of a piece of tissue where it is  laid down of top the array of ports and  I've outlined that tissue and a white  dotted line and then we're delivering a  fluorescent probe through a channel  beneath and you can just barely see the  top of that channel at the top of the  image and then when we see the drugs  starting to come up through the port in  this semi radial fashion from the port  when we draw a line scan across the  image we find that these distributions  tend to be pretty Gaussian as you see in  panel B and then we can fit that  Gaussian curve in order to find the area  under the curve and the width of the  curve and that enables us to get a sense  of how much are we delivering and we see  that if we deliver longer we get a  linear increase in the amount of dye  that's in the tissue and we get linear  increases in the width of the pulse at  least for these short delivery times  eventually it will saturate the tissue  so we've pretty good control over  delivering drugs through regions of us  that do and you'll notice there in panel  B for the shortest or pulse times a few  seconds we can get resolution on the  order of a couple hundreds of microns  depending on the size of the molecule  and so for these protein size molecules  we tend to have two or three hundred  micro resolution and that's sufficient  to target sub structures of tissue such  as in the lymph node say a b-cell  follicle or the T cell zone or in the  brain you might be targeting part of a  cortex for example we did some fluid  modeling to understand whether these  pulses are going all the way through the  tissue and so what you see here is a  model of the cross section of the device  so there's the channel on the bottom  with fluid flowing through it you can  see the arrows and then on top is  supposed to be a slab of tissue which we  modeled as a porous matrix and what  we've done here is we've  load for five seconds a pulse of a drug  and the concentration of that drug is  shown here in the color scale and now  we're starting to flow buffer in behind  it and so what we see is there's this  ballooning of a spot of drug pushing up  through the tissue and then if we wait  one minute we see that you know with  buffer coming in behind it's gone up and  out the top of the tissue and is  beginning to be washed away so the  delivery is passing all the way through  the tissue and that's a feature of this  design where we're not just stimulating  the surface of the tissue  we're actually penetrating and getting  all the way through the depth of this  thick slab of tissue okay so that was  the first generation of this chip with  these stationary ports beneath the  tissue one drawback of that chip design  is that it means that if you lay down  your tissue and you wanted to deliver  somewhere that didn't happen to fall  over one of the ten ports you would need  to move the tissue manually in order to  get it to align with the port and that  isn't too hard for a highly trained  scientist but for someone who might be  new or if you want to just have  technicians doing this you might damage  your tissue by having to move it so we  had a vision of changing the chip  designs so that you could lay down the  tissue and then move a court to be  anywhere that you wanted it to be and as  you can imagine you can't move anything  in a solid block of silicon rubber so  this required a complete redesign of the  chip and I had a talented grad student  who took this on and so she created what  is referred to as a slip chip design  where we have a piece of tissue lying  atop an array of ports at this time we  have over 80 ports beneath the tissue  and then that array instead of being  directly connected to a channel is now  floating on a layer of oil below that is  an additional channel and and then a  single port and I've shown that shown  filter with green so what do you know  about oil and water they don't mix so  this is basically keep  completely separated the solution of  drug that might be in that channel down  below from the Culture Media that is  surrounding the tissue and then what you  can do is slip and slide the two layers  since they're lubricated by this layer  of oil in order to connect the port of  interest above with the channel below  and then you can turn on a pump and  deliver up and now you have delivery  akin to what we had in the other chip  and then we can just connect again and  turn off the pump in order to turn off  the flow and so my student Meg worked  out all the physics of this I got it's a  lot it's based on surface tension to  keep the delivery fluid from leaking out  into that oilfield layer and we found  that we were able to deliver selectively  to regions of interest repeatedly within  the same tissues and so we're continuing  to work on this design to move  completely away from ports and into more  of a membrane format so that we can  deliver truly anywhere on demand okay so  what might one do with a system like  this where you can locally deliver one  of the first applications that we  decided to target was to ask how do  molecules move through different parts  of the tissue because we know that in  tissue you aren't diffusing in a simple  way instead you're experiencing what's  called hindered diffusion and where  molecules have to move through the  tortuous spaces between cells and this  has consequences ranging from  development and repair of organs to the  paracrine signaling between cells and  importantly to drug delivery as you  think about a drug that has exited the  bloodstream and needs to travel several  hundred microns before it reaches a a  tumor or a region of interest in the  tissue it's doing that by diffusion and  effusion is currently measured in  tissues by either using frapp  fluorescence recovery after  photobleaching where you soak your  entire tissue in a die and then bleach  once and watch as the molecule diffuses  back in that works well for inert probes  but won't work well for a bioactive  molecule that might change the tissue  during the soaking time so instead we  wanted to use local delivery and that is  you  really done by some sort of needle and  started into the tissue and then imaging  or electrochemistry to watch it spread  but again we're worried with an immune  tissue and perhaps with other tissues as  well then inserting a needle that is  tens or hundreds of microns in diameter  into the tissue could cause damage that  changes the transport behavior that we  want to watch so this was a great  application for this gentle fluidic  delivery and so we took the device that  I just shared with you the simple one  where I am everything is stationary and  we simplified it further so we have just  a single channel and port beneath the  tissue and the goal here is to deliver  bioactive molecules into the tissue and  then watch their spread so typical  experiment looks like this you see on  the left and image of a piece of tissue  that we've labeled the B cells which  tend to be around the edges in green and  then we're delivering a red fluorescent  protein in this case it's tumor necrosis  factor-alpha that we labeled with  rhodamine dry and then we deliver for  five seconds and you see that in that  second image which is labeled zero  minutes so that becomes time zero is  that pulse of dye that's been delivered  to the center then we just stop the pump  and we watch over time as the molecule  diffuses away then we can quantify by  image and analysis the spread of that  molecule and back out the diffusion  coefficient and we validated this  against frappe in inert gels with  standard probes and found that we're  within two fold of reported measurements  so we're fairly confident in these  numbers and then we were able to make  the first measurements of the diffusion  of Sanna alpha and other bioactive  proteins moving through different parts  