brane that restricted cell movement  by having small pores and that we were  using two micron for size membranes and  that is a design choice that can be  buried in the future in order to test  the effects of cells traveling for  example to measure metastasis so one of  the important features of this design  was to get the fluid flow to pass  through the tissue not just over it  because we want to pick up paracrine  signals and carry them to the downstream  tissue and so this is a result from a  model of fluid flow simulation and konso  multiphysics and there's the tissue  right there so you can see it appearing  and disappearing  and I'm Jo found that by placing an  inlet on the upper left and an outlet on  the lower right he was able to drive  fluid flow pretty much straight down  through that issue so that he could pick  up anything that was being secreted and  carry it out other designs where the  inlets were both on the same side or  both on the top or both on the bottom  really didn't provide this flow through  we fabricated this using standard PDMS  micro fluidics once again with a three  layer chip and a trackage membrane that  goes beneath the tissues in order to  hold them in place and restore some  movement and then we needed to validate  how proteins moved between slices and  whether proteins secreted by one tissue  could indeed be captured by another  tissue and so to do that we set up a  simple model system we made mock tissues  out of agarose and loaded them  separately with either a fluorescent  protein coupled to new travelens shown  on the top or by channel a to beat shown  on the bottom so this is to model or a  scepter ligand pair and we could  quantify the fluorescent intensity in  those maka tissues before the experiment  and of course only the protein loaded  slice as bright fluorescence and then we  cultured these overnight you know in the  incubator with continuous fluid flow the  way that we would do for its issues and  we saw that indeed all of that  fluorescent protein was captured by the  receptors in the other tissue after 24  hours with minimal loss to the device  and that was achieved by blocking the  the channels in a particular way with  ESA beforehand and if you're curious  about that you can look in the paper  okay so finally as a proof-of-concept we  had this chip that we knew could support  two slices of of gel or tissue and and  have proteins move between them and so  we asked to what extent can this model  some of the features of tumor immunity  and we started off with just one feature  which was that we know that in vivo  often the lymph nodes are  immunosuppressed by the tumor meaning  that the T cells can no longer respond  to really any type of stimulus very well  so we asked to what extent that could be  mimicked by the chip so we did an  experiment where we had a mouse that was  this is carrying a forty one breast  cancer tumor and then we collected its  lymph nodes and we did a measure of the  capacity of the T cells to respond to  stimulus we hit them with a anti cd3  antibody and we measure how much of a  cytokine called interferon gamma they  secreted and what you see is that naive  my secrete a lot of interferon gamma  where as lymph nodes from the tumor the  tumor bearing animal don't secrete much  interferon gamma at all and so we asked  could we replicate that on the chip so  we took lymph nodes that were from non  draining nodes from a tumor bearing  mouse and then coupled them with a tumor  and cultured overnight and for  comparison we took those same lymph  nodes other slices from those nodes and  cultured them with healthy tissue a fat  pad and we found that here culture with  the tumor again suppressed a response of  the t-cells  compared to the healthy fat so that was  really exciting because this is the  first indication of immuno suppression  on a chip with intact tissue and moving  forward we are hoping to further test  other features of immunosuppression and  and 